### What are the two liveness mechanisms in a Kafka consumer?

Modern Kafka consumers use two separate threads for liveness. A background heartbeat thread handles group membership, and the application thread handles polling and processing. This separation is the key to understanding these three settings.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class LivenessConfigDemo {
    public static Properties create() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");

        // Background heartbeat thread settings
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "3000");  // Wave every 3s
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS_CONFIG, "45000");    // Dead if no wave for 45s

        // Application thread setting
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "300000"); // Stalled if no poll for 5min

        System.out.println("Heartbeat interval: 3s (background thread)");
        System.out.println("Session timeout: 45s (dead consumer detection)");
        System.out.println("Max poll interval: 5min (stalled consumer detection)");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        create();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Heartbeat interval: 3s (background thread)
Session timeout: 45s (dead consumer detection)
Max poll interval: 5min (stalled consumer detection)
```

### How does heartbeat.interval.ms work?

The `heartbeat.interval.ms`, default three seconds, is how often the background thread sends a heartbeat to the group coordinator. Heartbeats are cheap and frequent, and they say the consumer process is alive and connected. You keep this well below the session timeout, commonly a third of it.

| Setting | Default | Thread | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| heartbeat.interval.ms | 3000 | Background | Send liveness signal |
| session.timeout.ms | 45000 | Background | Detect dead consumer |
| max.poll.interval.ms | 300000 | Application | Detect stalled consumer |

### How does session.timeout.ms detect a crashed consumer?

The `session.timeout.ms`, default forty-five seconds, is how long the coordinator waits without a heartbeat before declaring the consumer dead. This detects a crashed consumer, a killed process, or a network partition, because in those cases the heartbeat thread stops sending.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class SessionTimeoutScenario {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Scenario: settlement-consumer process killed");
        System.out.println("  t=0s:  Last heartbeat sent");
        System.out.println("  t=3s:  Heartbeat missed (process dead)");
        System.out.println("  t=6s:  Heartbeat missed again");
        System.out.println("  t=45s: Session timeout expires");
        System.out.println("  t=45s: Coordinator declares consumer dead");
        System.out.println("  t=45s: Rebalance triggered -- partitions reassigned");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Tuning: shorter timeout = faster detection, but risks false eviction");
        System.out.println("Tuning: longer timeout = more tolerant, but slower reaction");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Scenario: settlement-consumer process killed
  t=0s:  Last heartbeat sent
  t=3s:  Heartbeat missed (process dead)
  t=6s:  Heartbeat missed again
  t=45s: Session timeout expires
  t=45s: Coordinator declares consumer dead
  t=45s: Rebalance triggered -- partitions reassigned

Tuning: shorter timeout = faster detection, but risks false eviction
Tuning: longer timeout = more tolerant, but slower reaction
```

### How does max.poll.interval.ms detect a stalled consumer?

The `max.poll.interval.ms`, default five minutes, bounds the time between successive `poll()` calls on the application thread. If your handler takes longer than this, the consumer proactively leaves the group and its partitions are reassigned.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class PollIntervalScenario {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Scenario: slow compliance check blocks handler");
        System.out.println("  t=0s:   poll() returns 100 records");
        System.out.println("  t=10s:  Processing record 50, compliance API slow");
        System.out.println("  t=60s:  Still processing, heartbeats flowing normally");
        System.out.println("  t=180s: Still stuck on compliance call");
        System.out.println("  t=300s: max.poll.interval.ms (5 min) exceeded");
        System.out.println("  t=300s: Consumer leaves group -- rebalance triggered");
        System.out.println("  NOTE:  Heartbeats were flowing the entire time!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix: lower max.poll.records so batch finishes in time");
        System.out.println("Fix: or offload slow work to a worker thread");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Scenario: slow compliance check blocks handler
  t=0s:   poll() returns 100 records
  t=10s:  Processing record 50, compliance API slow
  t=60s:  Still processing, heartbeats flowing normally
  t=180s: Still stuck on compliance call
  t=300s: max.poll.interval.ms (5 min) exceeded
  t=300s: Consumer leaves group -- rebalance triggered
  NOTE:  Heartbeats were flowing the entire time!

Fix: lower max.poll.records so batch finishes in time
Fix: or offload slow work to a worker thread
```

### Why does a slow handler trigger rebalance even with heartbeats flowing?

The heartbeat thread runs independently on a background thread, so from the coordinator's membership view the consumer looks alive. But the application thread is stuck inside the handler and has not called `poll()` again within the poll interval. The two mechanisms answer different questions: is the process up (heartbeat), and is the processing progressing (poll).

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class TwoLivenessSignals {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Two independent liveness signals:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Background thread (heartbeat):");
        System.out.println("    Question: Is the process UP?");
        System.out.println("    Signal:   heartbeat.interval.ms");
        System.out.println("    Timeout:  session.timeout.ms");
        System.out.println("    Detects:  CRASHED consumer (killed, network down)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Application thread (poll):");
        System.out.println("    Question: Is processing PROGRESSING?");
        System.out.println("    Signal:   time between poll() calls");
        System.out.println("    Timeout:  max.poll.interval.ms");
        System.out.println("    Detects:  STALLED consumer (slow handler, deadlock)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Two independent liveness signals:

  Background thread (heartbeat):
    Question: Is the process UP?
    Signal:   heartbeat.interval.ms
    Timeout:  session.timeout.ms
    Detects:  CRASHED consumer (killed, network down)

  Application thread (poll):
    Question: Is processing PROGRESSING?
    Signal:   time between poll() calls
    Timeout:  max.poll.interval.ms
    Detects:  STALLED consumer (slow handler, deadlock)
```

### How do you tune these settings together?

Keep the heartbeat interval at about a third of the session timeout. Size max.poll.records and max.poll.interval.ms together so each batch finishes within the interval.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class TuningExample {
    public static Properties tunedConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        // Heartbeat: 1/3 of session timeout
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "10000");  // 10s
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS_CONFIG, "30000");     // 30s

        // Processing: batch size * processing time < poll interval
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, "50");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "600000");  // 10 min
        // 50 records * 10 sec each = 500 sec < 600 sec poll interval

        System.out.println("Tuned: heartbeat=10s, session=30s, poll-records=50, poll-interval=10min");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        tunedConfig();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Tuned: heartbeat=10s, session=30s, poll-records=50, poll-interval=10min
```

### What about pause and resume for long-running work?

For genuinely long work, offload it to a worker thread and use `consumer.pause()` and `consumer.resume()` to keep polling (and heartbeating) while the work proceeds, decoupling processing time from the poll interval.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Set;

public class PauseResumePattern {
    public static void handleSlowWork(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer) {
        Set<TopicPartition> assigned = consumer.assignment();

        // Pause fetching while slow work proceeds
        consumer.pause(assigned);
        System.out.println("Paused partitions -- poll() still called for heartbeats");

        // Submit slow work to a thread pool
        // ... worker processes the batch ...

        // Resume fetching when work completes
        consumer.resume(assigned);
        System.out.println("Resumed partitions -- fetching new records again");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

After a poll-interval eviction, partitions are reassigned and likely reprocessed, so idempotency matters. The reprocessing plus the rebalance itself can cascade if many consumers are slow. Doing heavy work on a separate thread with pause/resume is the pattern to decouple processing time from the poll interval.
